{
  "raw": {
    "https://faculty-emory.icims.com/jobs/174769/assistant-director%2c-cytogenetics-laboratory/job": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Cytogenetics Laboratory",
      "description": "Assistant Director, Cytogenetics Laboratory Job Number 174769 Job Type Regular Full-Time Division School Of Medicine Department SOM: Pathology: Admin Campus Location (For Posting) : Location US-GA-Atlanta Location : Name Emory Campus-Clifton Corridor Required Documents List of References Remote Work Classification N/A or Other Health and Safety Information Working with human blood, body fluids, tissues, or other potentially infectious materials Discover Your Career at Emory University Emory University is a leading research university that fosters excellence and attracts world-class talent to innovate today and prepare leaders for the future. We welcome candidates who can contribute to the excellence of our academic community. Description Assistant Director, Cytogenetics Laboratory Department of Pathology and Laboratory Medicine The Department of Pathology and Laboratory Medicine at Emory University School of Medicine is seeking two faculty members to serve as Assistant Directors of Cytogenetics. Successful candidates will join a collaborative, multidisciplinary team dedicated to clinical excellence, education, and genomic innovation. The Assistant Directors will partner with laboratory staff, pathologists, clinicians, and institutional stakeholders to enhance existing services and expand testing capabilities. This position provides opportunities for leadership in the cytogenetic lab, test development, education, research, and collaborative projects, supporting an academic career through clinical work, scholarship, and innovation. Academic rank will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. The Emory Cytogenetics Laboratory is a state-of-the-art diagnostic laboratory at Emory University Hospital, a leading academic medical center serving the Atlanta area. The laboratory offers a comprehensive range of cytogenetic and genomic diagnostic services, including constitutional and oncology cytogenetics, FISH, chromosomal copy number/SNP microarray, DNA methylation-based classification of CNS tumors, and NGS and emerging genomic technologies. Clinical Responsibilities The Assistant Directors will participate in the clinical operations and medical oversight of the Cytogenetics Laboratory, including: Reviewing, interpreting, and signing out clinical cytogenetic and molecular studies, including karyotype, FISH, chromosomal microarray, methylation, NGS, and other genomic testing Providing consultation to clinicians regarding test selection, interpretation, and follow-up testing Developing, validating, implementing, and optimizing new clinical assays and genomic technologies in collaboration with laboratory leadership, staff, and clinical stakeholders to expand clinical testing services Participating in quality assurance, regulatory compliance, and laboratory improvement initiatives, including troubleshooting technical and clinical issues Collaborating across pathology, genetics, oncology, pediatrics, and other clinical disciplines through multidisciplinary conferences, tumor boards, and other clinical activities Academic Opportunities Emory offers a rich academic environment supporting clinical innovation, teaching, research, and scholarship. Faculty will have opportunities to: Educate and mentor pathology residents, fellows, medical students, and other trainees Collaborate on clinical, translational, and genomic research across Emory University and Emory Healthcare Develop scholarly and quality-improvement initiatives and contribute to publications, abstracts, and presentations Advance the integration of cytogenetics, molecular diagnostics, and genomic medicine Qualifications D., D.O., Ph.D., or equivalent doctoral degree, with ABMGG board certification or eligibility in Laboratory Genetics and Genomics (LGG), or ABMGG board certification in Clinical Cytogenetics and Clinical Molecular Genetics/Genomics Expertise in clinical cytogenetics and/or genomic diagnostics, including chromosome analysis, FISH, chromosomal microarray, and molecular testing Experience with NGS, bioinformatics, genomic data analysis, variant interpretation, and clinical assay development is highly desirable Excellent communication, interpersonal, and teamwork skills, with the ability to collaborate effectively across laboratory, clinical, and academic teams As one of the nation's leading NIH-funded academic pathology departments, Emory provides an exceptional environment for collaboration, innovation, and academic growth. This is an on-site faculty position requiring regular participation in clinical, laboratory, educational, and academic activities. This position is not eligible for remote work. Application Please complete the online application and submit a curriculum vitae and names of three professional references to: John D. Roback, M.D., Ph.D. Executive Vice-Chair for Clinical Operations Medical Director, Emory Healthcare Medical Laboratories Department of Pathology and Laboratory Medicine Emory University Hospital, D-655 1364 Clifton Road Atlanta, GA 30322 jroback@emory.edu Additional Details Emory is an equal opportunity employer, and qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, protected veteran status or other characteristics protected by state or federal law. Emory University does not discriminate in admissions, educational programs, or employment, including recruitment, hiring, promotions, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits, and training. Students, faculty, and staff are assured of participation in university programs and in the use of facilities without such discrimination. Emory University complies with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, the Vietnam Era Veteran's Readjustment Assistance Act, and applicable executive orders, federal and state regulations regarding nondiscrimination, equal opportunity, and affirmative action (for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities). Inquiries regarding this policy should be directed to the Emory University Department of Equity and Civil Rights Compliance, 201 Dowman Drive, Administration Building, Atlanta, GA 30322. Telephone: 404-727-9867 (V) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). Emory University is committed to ensuring equal access and providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to seek a reasonable accommodation, please contact the Department of Accessibility Services at accessibility@emory.edu or call 404-727-9877 (Voice) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). We kindly ask that requests be made at least seven business days in advance to allow adequate time for coordination. Options Apply Now Apply Share Email this job to a friend Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Connect With Us! Not ready to apply? Connect with us for general consideration! Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://faculty-emory.icims.com/jobs/174649/assistant-professor---crt/job": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - CRT",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - CRT Job Number 174649 Job Type Regular Full-Time Division School Of Medicine Department SOM: Dermatology: Admin Campus Location (For Posting) : Location US-GA-Atlanta Location : Name Bldg 1525 Clifton Road Remote Work Classification Primarily On Campus Health and Safety Information Position involves clinical patient contact, Working with human blood, body fluids, tissues, or other potentially infectious materials Discover Your Career at Emory University Emory University is a leading research university that fosters excellence and attracts world-class talent to innovate today and prepare leaders for the future. We welcome candidates who can contribute to the excellence of our academic community. Description Assistant Professor – Dermatology (Academic Clinician-Researcher Track) Emory University School of Medicine, Department of Dermatology Position Summary The Department of Dermatology at Emory University School of Medicine seeks a board-certified or board-eligible dermatologist for a full-time faculty appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor on the Academic Clinician-Researcher Track. The successful candidate will provide comprehensive dermatologic care for adult patients, serve as a clinical dermatopathologist, and contribute to the academic, research, and educational missions of the Department. Primary Responsibilities Provide high-quality, evidence-based dermatologic care to adult patients in ambulatory and affiliated clinical settings. Perform clinical dermatopathology services and participate in multidisciplinary patient care activities. Develop and maintain an active program of clinical and/or translational research. Teach and mentor medical students, residents, fellows, and other trainees. Participate in departmental, institutional, and professional service activities. Contribute to scholarly publications, presentations, and other academic initiatives consistent with the expectations of the Academic Clinician-Researcher Track. Required Qualifications M.D., D.O., or equivalent medical degree. Board certification or board eligibility in Dermatology. Fellowship training and expertise in Dermatopathology. Eligibility for medical licensure in the State of Georgia. Demonstrated commitment to academic medicine, research, teaching, and patient care. NOTE: Position tasks are generally required to be performed in-person at an Emory University location. Remote work from home day options may be granted at department discretion. Emory reserves the right to change remote work status with notice to employee. Additional Details Emory is an equal opportunity employer, and qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, protected veteran status or other characteristics protected by state or federal law. Emory University does not discriminate in admissions, educational programs, or employment, including recruitment, hiring, promotions, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits, and training. Students, faculty, and staff are assured of participation in university programs and in the use of facilities without such discrimination. Emory University complies with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, the Vietnam Era Veteran's Readjustment Assistance Act, and applicable executive orders, federal and state regulations regarding nondiscrimination, equal opportunity, and affirmative action (for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities). Inquiries regarding this policy should be directed to the Emory University Department of Equity and Civil Rights Compliance, 201 Dowman Drive, Administration Building, Atlanta, GA 30322. Telephone: 404-727-9867 (V) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). Emory University is committed to ensuring equal access and providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to seek a reasonable accommodation, please contact the Department of Accessibility Services at accessibility@emory.edu or call 404-727-9877 (Voice) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). We kindly ask that requests be made at least seven business days in advance to allow adequate time for coordination. Options Apply Now Apply Share Email this job to a friend Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Connect With Us! Not ready to apply? Connect with us for general consideration! Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://faculty-emory.icims.com/jobs/174568/assistant-professor--medical-oncology--clinical-investigator/job": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor- Medical Oncology- Clinical Investigator",
      "description": "Assistant Professor- Medical Oncology- Clinical Investigator Job Number 174568 Job Type Regular Full-Time Division School Of Medicine Department SOM: HMO: Med Onc Campus Location (For Posting) : Location US-GA-Atlanta Location : Name Emory University Hospital Required Documents Transcripts Remote Work Classification Primarily On Campus Health and Safety Information Position involves clinical patient contact Discover Your Career at Emory University Emory University is a leading research university that fosters excellence and attracts world-class talent to innovate today and prepare leaders for the future. We welcome candidates who can contribute to the excellence of our academic community. Description The Department of Hematology and Medical Oncology, and the Emory Winship Cancer Institute in Atlanta, Georgia seeks a board-certified/board-eligible Neuro-Oncologist or Medical Oncologist with a specialty in Neuro/Brain Medical Oncology to join our rapidly growing Winship Brain Tumor Center. Applicants must be eligible for licensure in Georgia. Interested candidates should embrace team-oriented clinical care. The ideal candidate will provide direct patient care and support graduate medical education training and clinical research efforts at Emory Hospital at Midtown and/or Clifton Road. A track record of clinical research and scholarly activity is preferred, but not obligatory. Academic rank, compensation and appointments will be commensurate with applicant’s experience and qualifications. Academic interests outside of direct patient-care delivery are encouraged and supported. Efforts of particular interest include translational research, early phase clinical trials, central nervous system metastases, symptom management, observational studies, and/or health disparities research. The Department of Hematology and Medical Oncology, Division of Medical Oncology has a fast-growing faculty with professional practices at multiple locations in metro Atlanta. The Winship Cancer Institute of Emory University is a National Cancer Institute designated comprehensive cancer center. Hematology and Medical Oncology is a named department in the Emory School of Medicine, and its physician members deliver medical services in Emory Clinic, Inc. and other practice plans. Programs and services are located at Emory University Hospital, Emory University Hospital Midtown, Emory Decatur Hospital, Emory Saint Joseph’s Hospital, and Emory Johns Creek Hospital, and outside Emory Healthcare at the Grady Health System and the Atlanta Veterans’ Administration Medical Center. The Winship at Emory Midtown facility, is a state-of-the-art facility comprising 17 stories and more than 450,000 square feet, built around a patient's needs and comfort, bringing all the necessary doctors, staff and services to the patient. The care communities focus on patients with specific types of cancer, providing specialized infusion facilities, clinical examination rooms, clinical research, and spaces for support and rehabilitation services. NOTE: Position tasks are generally required to be performed in-person at an Emory University location. Remote work from home day options may be granted at department discretion. Emory reserves the right to change remote work status with notice to employee. Additional Details Emory is an equal opportunity employer, and qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, protected veteran status or other characteristics protected by state or federal law. Emory University does not discriminate in admissions, educational programs, or employment, including recruitment, hiring, promotions, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits, and training. Students, faculty, and staff are assured of participation in university programs and in the use of facilities without such discrimination. Emory University complies with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, the Vietnam Era Veteran's Readjustment Assistance Act, and applicable executive orders, federal and state regulations regarding nondiscrimination, equal opportunity, and affirmative action (for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities). Inquiries regarding this policy should be directed to the Emory University Department of Equity and Civil Rights Compliance, 201 Dowman Drive, Administration Building, Atlanta, GA 30322. Telephone: 404-727-9867 (V) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). Emory University is committed to ensuring equal access and providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to seek a reasonable accommodation, please contact the Department of Accessibility Services at accessibility@emory.edu or call 404-727-9877 (Voice) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). We kindly ask that requests be made at least seven business days in advance to allow adequate time for coordination. Options Apply Now Apply Share Email this job to a friend Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Connect With Us! Not ready to apply? Connect with us for general consideration! Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://faculty-emory.icims.com/jobs/174536/post-doctoral-fellow/job": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Post Doctoral Fellow Job Number 174536 Job Type Regular Full-Time Division School Of Public Health Department SPH: Epidemiology Campus Location (For Posting) : Location US-GA-Atlanta Location : Name Emory Campus-Clifton Corridor Required Documents Biosketch, Cover letter, List of Recommenders Remote Work Classification Primarily Remote – Monthly Health and Safety Information Not Applicable Discover Your Career at Emory University Emory University is a leading research university that fosters excellence and attracts world-class talent to innovate today and prepare leaders for the future. We welcome candidates who can contribute to the excellence of our academic community. Description Dr. Angela Bengtson ( https://sph.emory.edu/profile/faculty/angela-bengtson) within the Department of Epidemiology, Rollins School of Public Health (RSPH), Emory University is seeking a post-doctoral fellow in Epidemiology. The postdoctoral fellow will join a dynamic research group focused on improving health outcomes for women living with HIV and their children. The post-doc will support a range of studies based in South Africa, Malawi, and the US with a focus on comorbidities during the perinatal, postpartum, and pediatric periods. Key responsibilities: • Work closely with the Principal Investigator in all aspects of the research • Conduct data analysis using quantitative epidemiological methods using SAS, Stata, or R • Develop manuscripts for publication in peer-reviewed journals, as a co-author and first author • Support or lead grant proposal development • Present findings at scientific conferences • Support project implementation, including overseeing data collection, recruitment, retention, regulatory issues, and data management activities The post-doc will be expected to support data analysis and manuscript development for multiple NIH funded R01 studies focused on HIV exposed children in South Africa and improving perinatal depression among women with HIV in Malawi. The post-doc will also be expected to support project coordination for a study in South Africa to support healthy gestational weight gain for women with and without HIV. These activities are expected to result in multiple opportunities for leading or co-authoring scientific manuscripts, developing grant proposals, supporting project implantation, and presentation at scientific conferences. The ideal candidate will have a demonstrated interest in pediatric or perinatal epidemiology and experience in HIV research, excellent quantitative and analytic skills, and experience with project coordination, including overseeing study recruitment, retention, regulatory, and data management activities. Candidates are required to commit at least one year to the position, with the opportunity to extend to a second year. Candidates with a demonstrated interest in pursuing a career in academic research are strongly preferred, including those interested in developing a K award or other research grant proposals. The fellow will be under the mentorship of Principal Investigator Dr. Angela Bengtson and will have the opportunity to collaborate with investigators at Emory in RSPH and Emory School of Medicine, as well as collaborators South Africa and Malawi. The fellow will be based at Emory University in Atlanta, Georgia, with a start date of Fall 2026. Salary and benefits will be commensurate with NIH standards. JOB DESCRIPTION: Helps design and conduct research within a specified field while receiving advanced training from a designated Principal Investigator to enhance professional skills and research independence needed for pursuit of a career. The specific area of research in which the trainee is mentored is determined by the department and laboratory of the Postdoc. Designs and evaluates experiments. Develops new ideas that promote current research. Prepares and publishes scientific manuscripts under the direction of the Principal Investigator. May be responsible for operation of specific equipment. May teach techniques to others, train, and supervise research staff. Positions are temporary appointments as a research trainee. The initial appointment is for one year, renewal expected if progress is satisfactory and funds are available. Appointments cannot exceed five years. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: A doctoral degree or equivalent (Ph.D., M.D., ScD., D.V.M., DDS etc) in an appropriate field. Excellent scientific writing ability and strong oral communication skills. The ability to work effectively and collegially with colleagues. Additional qualifications as specified by the Principal Investigator. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: PhD in Epidemiology, Public Health, Behavioral Health (with excellent quantitative analytic skills) or other relevant quantitative field Expertise in epidemiologic methods; experience in longitudinal data analysis, predictive modeling, or advanced epidemiology methods is preferred Experience in HIV, global health, or pediatric/perinatal research Excellent scientific writing ability with a demonstrated publication record Experience in data management and analysis using SAS, R or other statistical software. Experience with project coordination for HIV or global health projects, including overseeing study recruitment, retention, and data management Excellent interpersonal skills with the ability to foster a good working relationship with others on the team and collaborators, including cross-culturally Strong interest in a career in academic research Ability to commit at least one year to the position, with a strong interest in two years. NOTE: This role will be granted the opportunity to work from home regularly but must be able to commute to Emory University location as needed and at times, on short notice. Emory reserves the right to change this status with notice to employee. Additional Details Emory is an equal opportunity employer, and qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, protected veteran status or other characteristics protected by state or federal law. Emory University does not discriminate in admissions, educational programs, or employment, including recruitment, hiring, promotions, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits, and training. Students, faculty, and staff are assured of participation in university programs and in the use of facilities without such discrimination. Emory University complies with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, the Vietnam Era Veteran's Readjustment Assistance Act, and applicable executive orders, federal and state regulations regarding nondiscrimination, equal opportunity, and affirmative action (for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities). Inquiries regarding this policy should be directed to the Emory University Department of Equity and Civil Rights Compliance, 201 Dowman Drive, Administration Building, Atlanta, GA 30322. Telephone: 404-727-9867 (V) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). Emory University is committed to ensuring equal access and providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to seek a reasonable accommodation, please contact the Department of Accessibility Services at accessibility@emory.edu or call 404-727-9877 (Voice) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). We kindly ask that requests be made at least seven business days in advance to allow adequate time for coordination. Options Apply Now Apply Share Email this job to a friend Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Connect With Us! Not ready to apply? Connect with us for general consideration! Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://faculty-emory.icims.com/jobs/174461/faculty-position---open-rank-in-native-american-and-indigenous-studies/job": {
      "title": "Faculty Position - Open Rank in Native American and Indigenous Studies",
      "description": "Faculty Position - Open Rank in Native American and Indigenous Studies Job Number 174461 Job Type Regular Full-Time Division Emory College Department ECAS: Dean of the College Campus Location (For Posting) : Location US-GA-Atlanta Location : Name Emory Campus-Clifton Corridor Required Documents Cover letter, Research Statement Remote Work Classification No Remote Health and Safety Information Not Applicable Discover Your Career at Emory University Emory University is a leading research university that fosters excellence and attracts world-class talent to innovate today and prepare leaders for the future. We welcome candidates who can contribute to the excellence of our academic community. Description ****DO NOT APPLY HERE**** +++ To learn more about this position and to apply, please visit https://apply.interfolio.com/194090 +++ Assistant, Associate or Full Professor in Native American and Indigenous Studies The Center for Native American and Indigenous Studies at Emory University seeks applications from scholars with expertise in Native American and Indigenous Studies, at the rank of Assistant, Associate or Full Professor. We understand Indigenous Studies to be simultaneously regional, hemispheric, and global while always anchored by the epistemologies of, and responsibilities to, those on whose lands we are located. We welcome applicants who collaborate with Indigenous communities in North America, South America, and the Pacific. We are searching for two scholars of any rank, including those currently completing PhDs, to fill disciplinary needs in a variety of areas that serve our planned minor in NAIS. We seek scholars who focus on Indigenous research and methods at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, and who have a critical, interdisciplinary orientation spanning the humanities, social sciences, fine arts and STEM fields, including the study of health, critical theory, the environment, literature, politics, law, history, arts and culture, sovereignty, the study of Nation building, race, gender and/or sexuality. All applicants must have a demonstrated commitment to teaching and mentoring a diverse student body. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in a relevant field at the time of appointment. Successful candidates at the Associate or Full level will be at the forefront of current scholarship that is redefining Indigenous Studies and rewriting academic, legal, and/or popular narratives of Indigenous peoples, with a national and/or international reputation. At the level of Assistant Professor, successful candidates will show strong records of scholarly achievement and the potential for leadership in Indigenous Studies. The successful candidates will be active scholars who are grounded in Indigenous knowledge systems and can demonstrate evidence of their commitment to working with Indigenous students and communities. As members of our Native American and Indigenous Studies Academic Learning Community, the candidates will contribute to shaping the future of Native American and Indigenous Studies at Emory, where faculty, staff, and students are building an initiative that will have impact for generations to come. At Emory University, Native American and Indigenous Studies is a University initiative located within the Emory College of Arts and Sciences that prioritizes partnerships with Indigenous communities and leads curricular programs, including co-teaching, with faculty at the College of the Muscogee Nation. Our Academic Learning Community includes faculty from both Emory College and Oxford College in Music, Art history, English, History, Environmental Sciences, Neuroscience, Italian Studies, and Political Science. Focusing on visibility, representation in the curriculum, and enhancing faculty research, this collaborative work aims to cultivate meaningful relationships with local communities and those who have traditional or historic relationships to Emory’s campuses, especially Indigenous communities of the Southeast. Programmatic leadership experience that takes place within community and academic spaces is welcomed. In the last five years, Emory has hired five new faculty members in this field and established the Center for Native American and Indigenous Studies, funded in part by a Mellon Foundation grant that also supports Muscogee language teaching and preservation at the College of the Muscogee Nation. The search committee will begin review of applications on November 15, 2026, with applications being accepted until the positions are filled. At this time, we ask applicants to submit the following to https://apply.interfolio.com/194090 : a cover letter a CV a research statement a teaching statement In your materials, please address your scholarly research, teaching experience, and any experience you may have with the following: Engaging Indigenous epistemologies, frameworks and perspectives in research and pedagogy; Collaborating directly with Indigenous communities on research or applied sovereignty projects; Working as part of an interdisciplinary academic community to enhance Indigenous studies as a discipline; Supporting the success of Indigenous students, faculty and staff in academic environments, including tribal colleges and universities; Interest in or experience with cross appointments with professional schools, such as law, public health, theology, or business. Candidates will be asked to submit additional supporting materials, including writing samples and letters of recommendation, if selected to participate further in the recruitment process. Emory University Land Acknowledgement Emory University acknowledges the Muscogee (Creek) people who lived, worked, produced knowledge on, and nurtured the land where Emory’s Oxford and Atlanta campuses are now located. In 1821, fifteen years before Emory’s founding, the Muscogee were forced to relinquish this land. We recognize the sustained oppression, land dispossession, and involuntary removals of the Muscogee and Cherokee peoples from Georgia and the Southeast. Emory seeks to honor the Muscogee Nation and other Indigenous caretakers of this land by humbly seeking knowledge of their histories and committing to respectful stewardship of the land. More at: https://www.emory.edu/about/history/land-acknowledgment NOTE: Position tasks are required to be performed in-person at an Emory University location; working remote is not an option. Emory reserves the right to change this status with notice to employee. ****DO NOT APPLY HERE**** Additional Details Emory is an equal opportunity employer, and qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, protected veteran status or other characteristics protected by state or federal law. Emory University does not discriminate in admissions, educational programs, or employment, including recruitment, hiring, promotions, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits, and training. Students, faculty, and staff are assured of participation in university programs and in the use of facilities without such discrimination. Emory University complies with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, the Vietnam Era Veteran's Readjustment Assistance Act, and applicable executive orders, federal and state regulations regarding nondiscrimination, equal opportunity, and affirmative action (for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities). Inquiries regarding this policy should be directed to the Emory University Department of Equity and Civil Rights Compliance, 201 Dowman Drive, Administration Building, Atlanta, GA 30322. Telephone: 404-727-9867 (V) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). Emory University is committed to ensuring equal access and providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to seek a reasonable accommodation, please contact the Department of Accessibility Services at accessibility@emory.edu or call 404-727-9877 (Voice) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). We kindly ask that requests be made at least seven business days in advance to allow adequate time for coordination. Options Apply Now Apply Share Email this job to a friend Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Connect With Us! Not ready to apply? Connect with us for general consideration! Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
