    "https://faculty-emory.icims.com/jobs/172540/assistant-professor%2c-political-science/job": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Political Science",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Political Science Job Number 172540 Job Type Regular Full-Time Division Emory College Department ECAS: Political Science Campus Location (For Posting) : Location US-GA-Atlanta Location : Name Emory Campus-Clifton Corridor Remote Work Classification Primarily On Campus Health and Safety Information Not Applicable Discover Your Career at Emory University Emory University is a leading research university that fosters excellence and attracts world-class talent to innovate today and prepare leaders for the future. We welcome candidates who can contribute to the excellence of our academic community. Description *** DO NOT APPLY HERE *** All materials should be submitted online through Interfolio by visiting our application website at https://apply.interfolio.com/191227 . AMERICAN GOVERNMENT AND POLITICS Emory University Rank: Assistant Professor, Political Science The Emory University Department of Political Science is recruiting for a tenure-track assistant professor position in American politics, with an expected start date of August 1, 2027. We welcome applications from scholars with a broad range of substantive foci and methodological approaches to the study of American politics. Job Description . The Department of Political Science, a leading community of scholars dedicated to excellence in research and teaching, invites applications for a tenure-track faculty appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor with a specialization in American politics. We are interested in scholars whose research is grounded in American politics, theoretically informed, and methodologically rigorous. The position is open with regard to research specialty within American politics. Candidates are expected to teach undergraduate and graduate courses, mentor students, perform service appropriate to rank, and conduct research. Salary and research support are competitive. Qualifications : Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in Political Science or a related field or be in the final stages of completing their dissertation. Application Instructions . A complete application must include a cover letter, a statement describing current and future research, a curriculum vitae, a writing sample, at least three recommendation letters, a graduate transcript, and a statement of teaching philosophy and experience. Do not apply via ICIMS. All materials should be submitted online through Interfolio by visiting our application website at https://apply.interfolio.com/191227 . Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Please contact Michelle Brown, Program Coordinator, at michelle.brown@emory.edu, with any questions about the application process. NOTE: Position tasks are generally required to be performed in-person at an Emory University location. Remote work from home day options may be granted at department discretion. Emory reserves the right to change remote work status with notice to employee. Additional Details Emory is an equal opportunity employer, and qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, protected veteran status or other characteristics protected by state or federal law. Emory University does not discriminate in admissions, educational programs, or employment, including recruitment, hiring, promotions, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits, and training. Students, faculty, and staff are assured of participation in university programs and in the use of facilities without such discrimination. Emory University complies with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, the Vietnam Era Veteran's Readjustment Assistance Act, and applicable executive orders, federal and state regulations regarding nondiscrimination, equal opportunity, and affirmative action (for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities). Inquiries regarding this policy should be directed to the Emory University Department of Equity and Civil Rights Compliance, 201 Dowman Drive, Administration Building, Atlanta, GA 30322. Telephone: 404-727-9867 (V) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). Emory University is committed to ensuring equal access and providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to seek a reasonable accommodation, please contact the Department of Accessibility Services at accessibility@emory.edu or call 404-727-9877 (Voice) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). We kindly ask that requests be made at least seven business days in advance to allow adequate time for coordination. Options Apply Now Apply Share Email this job to a friend Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Connect With Us! Not ready to apply? Connect with us for general consideration! Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://faculty-emory.icims.com/jobs/172539/faculty-position---open-rank/job": {
      "title": "Faculty Position - Open Rank",
      "description": "Faculty Position - Open Rank Job Number 172539 Job Type Regular Full-Time Division School Of Medicine Department SOM: Rehab: DPT Program Campus Location (For Posting) : Location US-GA-Atlanta Location : Name Emory Rehab Hospital Remote Work Classification Primarily On Campus Health and Safety Information Working in a laboratory Discover Your Career at Emory University Emory University is a leading research university that fosters excellence and attracts world-class talent to innovate today and prepare leaders for the future. We welcome candidates who can contribute to the excellence of our academic community. Description The Division of Physical Therapy within the Department of Rehabilitation Medicine at Emory University School of Medicine invites applications for a full-time research faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor, depending on qualifications and experience. The successful candidate will join a nationally recognized Division of Physical Therapy that is committed to advancing rehabilitation science, improving health and function across diverse populations, and fostering interdisciplinary collaboration. The Division is home to a vibrant community of clinician-scientists, educators, and researchers engaged in innovative research spanning movement science, neurologic rehabilitation, aging, neuroplasticity, mobility, exercise and health promotion, and rehabilitation technology. The position offers the opportunity to collaborate with faculty across Emory University, Emory Healthcare, the Rollins School of Public Health, Georgia Institute of Technology, the Center for Physical Therapy and Movement Science, and numerous clinical and research partners throughout the Atlanta region. The Division is particularly interested in candidates whose research complements and expands existing strengths while fostering new interdisciplinary collaborations across rehabilitation medicine, public health, engineering, neuroscience, data science, and implementation science. Applicants with an established or emerging record of NIH-funded research and a vision for developing innovative translational and community-impact research programs are especially encouraged to apply. Responsibilities: The successful candidate will be expected to: • Develop and maintain an independent, externally funded program of research. • Disseminate scholarly work through peer-reviewed publications and national and international presentations. • Collaborate with faculty, trainees, clinicians, and interdisciplinary investigators across Emory University and affiliated institutions. • Contribute to the mentorship of graduate students, postdoctoral fellows, residents, and junior faculty. • Participate in teaching and mentoring activities commensurate with a research-focused appointment, including contributions to the DPT and PhD curricula and supervision of student research and scholarly projects. • Participate in departmental, divisional, university, and professional service activities. • Contribute to the research mission and strategic growth of the Division of Physical Therapy and Department of Rehabilitation Medicine. Required Qualifications • Earned PhD (or equivalent research doctorate) in rehabilitation science, movement science, neuroscience, kinesiology, epidemiology, biomedical engineering, public health, or a related field. • Evidence of a productive program of scholarship, including peer-reviewed publications. • Demonstrated potential for obtaining and sustaining extramural research funding. • Strong commitment to collaborative and interdisciplinary research. Preferred Qualifications • Eligibility for physical therapist licensure in the State of Georgia. • Record of successful extramural funding as Principal Investigator or Co-Investigator. • Experience conducting clinically relevant rehabilitation research. • Interest in mentoring doctoral students, postdoctoral fellows, residents, and early-career investigators. • Strong commitment to broadening opportunities in research and academic environments. About the Division The Division of Physical Therapy at Emory University is nationally recognized for excellence in education, discovery, and service. Faculty within the Division lead innovative research programs focused on rehabilitation, neuroplasticity, mobility, aging, and movement science while maintaining strong collaborations across the School of Medicine, Emory Healthcare, and the broader scientific community. The Division is committed to cultivating the well-being individuals and communities through exemplary leadership in physical therapy education, discovery, and service. Application Materials Applicants should submit as a single PDF: Cover letter describing research interests and career goals (up to 1 page) Curriculum vitae Research statement (up to 3 pages) Statement describing mentorship, teaching, and/or service interests (up to 2 pages) Contact information for three professional references For questions concerning the position, contact Michael Borich at michael.borich@emory.edu. Additional Details Emory is an equal opportunity employer, and qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, protected veteran status or other characteristics protected by state or federal law. Emory University does not discriminate in admissions, educational programs, or employment, including recruitment, hiring, promotions, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits, and training. Students, faculty, and staff are assured of participation in university programs and in the use of facilities without such discrimination. Emory University complies with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, the Vietnam Era Veteran's Readjustment Assistance Act, and applicable executive orders, federal and state regulations regarding nondiscrimination, equal opportunity, and affirmative action (for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities). Inquiries regarding this policy should be directed to the Emory University Department of Equity and Civil Rights Compliance, 201 Dowman Drive, Administration Building, Atlanta, GA 30322. Telephone: 404-727-9867 (V) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). Emory University is committed to ensuring equal access and providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to seek a reasonable accommodation, please contact the Department of Accessibility Services at accessibility@emory.edu or call 404-727-9877 (Voice) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). We kindly ask that requests be made at least seven business days in advance to allow adequate time for coordination. Options Apply Now Apply Share Email this job to a friend Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Connect With Us! Not ready to apply? Connect with us for general consideration! Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://faculty-emory.icims.com/jobs/172422/faculty-psychologist---grady-ponce-center/job": {
      "title": "Faculty Psychologist - Grady Ponce Center",
      "description": "Faculty Psychologist - Grady Ponce Center Job Number 172422 Job Type Regular Full-Time Division School Of Medicine Department SOM: Psych: Admin Campus Location (For Posting) : Location US-GA-Atlanta Location : Name Grady Hospital Remote Work Classification Primarily On Campus Health and Safety Information Position involves clinical patient contact Discover Your Career at Emory University Emory University is a leading research university that fosters excellence and attracts world-class talent to innovate today and prepare leaders for the future. We welcome candidates who can contribute to the excellence of our academic community. Description The Emory University Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences is seeking a faculty psychologist to join the behavioral health team at Grady Health System's Ponce De Leon Center. The Ponce Center is one of the nation's largest and most comprehensive HIV prevention and care programs providing interdisciplinary care for more than 10,000 adults and youth living with or affected by HIV. Ponce partners with community-based organizations throughout metro Atlanta to address the complex medical, behavioral health, and social needs of its patients. This position is 100% focused on those living with HIV at Ponce (~7,000). The ideal candidate will have expertise in clinical, clinical health, or counseling psychology, integrated behavioral health, and the provision of culturally responsive care for individuals living with chronic medical conditions. The psychologist will conduct comprehensive assessments and provide evidence-informed and culturally responsive psychological interventions to support adults, and ideally youth as well, in managing the emotional, behavioral, and social challenges associated with HIV and co-occurring medical and psychiatric conditions. The psychologist will work collaboratively within an interdisciplinary team that includes physicians, advanced practice providers, nurses, social workers, pharmacists, case managers, dental staff, and community partners. In addition to clinical responsibilities, the psychologist will participate in scholarship, which may include interprofessional research focused on HIV care, health outcomes, health disparities, and behavioral health interventions. The faculty member will also contribute to the education and supervision of psychology trainees, psychiatry residents, medical students, and other health professional learners. Applicants should: 1. Have experience working in interdisciplinary teams in medical settings serving medically complex populations as well as ideally in integrated care contexts 2. Hold a doctorate in Clinical or Counseling Psychology from an APA-accredited graduate program and have completed an APA-accredited internship and at least one year of supervised postdoctoral training 3. Be licensed or license eligible as a psychologist in Georgia 4. Obtain board certification through the American Board of Professional Psychology (ABPP) within five years of appointment A successful candidate will demonstrate expertise in: • Comprehensive assessment of individuals living with HIV and/or other chronic medical conditions • Behavioral interventions that promote medication adherence, engagement in HIV care, health behavior change, and disease self-management • Treatment and management of co-occurring medical and mental health conditions, including depression, anxiety, trauma- and stressor-related disorders, substance use disorders, and serious mental illness • Evidence-informed interventions • Trauma-informed and culturally responsive care for individuals from diverse cultural, socioeconomic, racial, ethnic, gender, and sexual identity backgrounds • Psychosocial factors that affect health outcomes, including stigma, discrimination, housing instability, social isolation, and other social determinants of health • Interprofessional teamwork, collaboration, and integrated care delivery • Clinical teaching, supervision, and mentorship of trainees • Research related to caring for medically complex populations, behavioral health, health disparities, implementation science, or related areas This faculty position offers competitive compensation, comprehensive benefits, and numerous opportunities for professional growth, leadership, teaching, and research. Join us in advancing innovative, patient-centered HIV care, reducing health disparities, and improving the lives of individuals and communities affected by HIV. NOTE: Position tasks are generally required to be performed in-person at an Emory University location. Remote work from home day options may be granted at department discretion. Emory reserves the right to change remote work status with notice to employee. Additional Details Emory is an equal opportunity employer, and qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, protected veteran status or other characteristics protected by state or federal law. Emory University does not discriminate in admissions, educational programs, or employment, including recruitment, hiring, promotions, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits, and training. Students, faculty, and staff are assured of participation in university programs and in the use of facilities without such discrimination. Emory University complies with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, the Vietnam Era Veteran's Readjustment Assistance Act, and applicable executive orders, federal and state regulations regarding nondiscrimination, equal opportunity, and affirmative action (for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities). Inquiries regarding this policy should be directed to the Emory University Department of Equity and Civil Rights Compliance, 201 Dowman Drive, Administration Building, Atlanta, GA 30322. Telephone: 404-727-9867 (V) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). Emory University is committed to ensuring equal access and providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to seek a reasonable accommodation, please contact the Department of Accessibility Services at accessibility@emory.edu or call 404-727-9877 (Voice) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). We kindly ask that requests be made at least seven business days in advance to allow adequate time for coordination. Options Apply Now Apply Share Email this job to a friend Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Connect With Us! Not ready to apply? Connect with us for general consideration! Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://faculty-emory.icims.com/jobs/172317/post-doctoral-fellow-%28emory-vaccine-center%29/job": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Fellow (Emory Vaccine Center)",
      "description": "Post Doctoral Fellow (Emory Vaccine Center) Job Number 172317 Job Type Regular Full-Time Division Emory Nat'l Biomedical Res Ctr Department EBC: Emory Vaccine Center Campus Location (For Posting) : Location US-GA-Atlanta Location : Name Emory Campus-Clifton Corridor Remote Work Classification No Remote Health and Safety Information Potential exposure to hazardous chemicals, Working with human blood, body fluids, tissues, or other potentially infectious materials, Working with nonhuman primates (nhp), nhp tissue, body fluids or equipment contaminated by nhp Discover Your Career at Emory University Emory University is a leading research university that fosters excellence and attracts world-class talent to innovate today and prepare leaders for the future. We welcome candidates who can contribute to the excellence of our academic community. Description The laboratory of Dr. Rui Kong within the Emory Vaccine Center at the Emory National Biomedical Research Center (ENBRC) of Emory University in Atlanta, GA, is currently seeking highly motivated Post Doctoral Fellows. Research in our lab focuses on therapeutic monoclonal antibody discovery, vaccine development, and B cell responses. We are interested in the following topics: 1) Discovery of diagnostic, preventive, and therapeutic monoclonal antibodies targeting diseases across a broad range of biomedical fields; 2) Vaccine development against HIV-1 and other infectious diseases; 3) B cell immune responses to infection and vaccination; 4) Monoclonal antibody discovery technologies. Dr. Rui Kong is an Assistant Professor in the Emory Vaccine Center and Emory National Biomedical Research Center, Assistant Professor in the Department of Pathology and Laboratory Medicine, Emory University School of Medicine, and Georgia Research Alliance Distinguished Investigator. Previous publications from Dr. Rui Kong can be found at the following link . The Emory Vaccine Center is an epicenter of academic research and development of vaccines for infectious diseases. With more than 250 faculty members and staff, it is the largest and most comprehensive academic vaccine research center in the world. The qualified candidate will work in a collaborative environment that is rich in resources, alongside investigators, graduate students, technical staff, and postdoctoral fellows conducting fundamental, basic, and clinical research leading to the development of effective vaccines against diseases of global importance. Candidates with backgrounds in immunology, virology, structural biology, bioinformatics, molecular biology, and animal models are highly encouraged to apply. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES: Helps design and conduct research within a specified field while receiving advanced training from a designated Principal Investigator to enhance professional skills and research independence needed for pursuit of a career. The specific area of research in which the trainee is mentored is determined by the department and laboratory of the Postdoc. Designs and evaluates experiments. Develops new ideas that promote current research. Prepares and publishes scientific manuscripts under the direction of the Principal Investigator. May be responsible for operation of specific equipment. May teach techniques to others, train, and supervise research staff. Positions are temporary appointments as a research trainee. The initial appointment is for one year, renewal expected if progress is satisfactory and funds are available. Appointments cannot exceed five years. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: A doctoral degree or equivalent (Ph.D., M.D., ScD., D.V.M., DDS etc) in an appropriate field. Excellent scientific writing ability and strong oral communication skills. The ability to work effectively and collegially with colleagues. Additional qualifications as specified by the Principal Investigator. PREFERRED/DESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: Excellent interpersonal and written and verbal communication skills WORKING ENVIRONMENT: Work environment involves working with biohazardous blood and tissues Work environment involves some exposure to hazards or physical risks, which require following basic safety precautions Work is normally performed in a typical laboratory environment Requires the ability to bend, kneel, and/or squat PRE-EMPLOYMENT SCREENING STATEMENT: The Emory National Biomedical Research Center (ENBRC), in conjunction with Emory University, conducts pre-employment screenings for all positions. These screenings may include an Information Network Associates (INA) and criminal background check, and verification of work history, academic credentials, licenses, and certifications. In addition, the process may include a drug screening with health assessment, and review and mandate for various vaccinations. Updated COVID-19 vaccinations may be necessary for specific research projects. NOTE: Position tasks are generally required to be performed in-person at an Emory University location. Remote work from home day options may be granted at department discretion. Emory reserves the right to change remote work status with notice to employee. Additional Details Emory is an equal opportunity employer, and qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, protected veteran status or other characteristics protected by state or federal law. Emory University does not discriminate in admissions, educational programs, or employment, including recruitment, hiring, promotions, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits, and training. Students, faculty, and staff are assured of participation in university programs and in the use of facilities without such discrimination. Emory University complies with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, the Vietnam Era Veteran's Readjustment Assistance Act, and applicable executive orders, federal and state regulations regarding nondiscrimination, equal opportunity, and affirmative action (for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities). Inquiries regarding this policy should be directed to the Emory University Department of Equity and Civil Rights Compliance, 201 Dowman Drive, Administration Building, Atlanta, GA 30322. Telephone: 404-727-9867 (V) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). Emory University is committed to ensuring equal access and providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to seek a reasonable accommodation, please contact the Department of Accessibility Services at accessibility@emory.edu or call 404-727-9877 (Voice) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). We kindly ask that requests be made at least seven business days in advance to allow adequate time for coordination. Options Apply Now Apply Share Email this job to a friend Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Connect With Us! Not ready to apply? Connect with us for general consideration! Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://faculty-emory.icims.com/jobs/172214/post-doctoral-fellow/job": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Post Doctoral Fellow Job Number 172214 Job Type Regular Full-Time Division School Of Medicine Department SOM: Medicine: Infectious Dis Campus Location (For Posting) : Location US-GA-Decatur Location : Name Hope Clinic Remote Work Classification Hybrid Remote Health and Safety Information Not Applicable Discover Your Career at Emory University Emory University is a leading research university that fosters excellence and attracts world-class talent to innovate today and prepare leaders for the future. We welcome candidates who can contribute to the excellence of our academic community. Description The Collins Lab is recruiting a postdoctoral researcher to investigate key topics in translational arbovirology, including flavivirus immune interactions, vaccine development, applied epidemiology, and clinical trials. The fellow will lead projects focused on human antibody responses elicited by infection and/or vaccination, towards the goals of developing and implementing tools, interventions, and therapeutics to detect and prevent arbovirus infection. The work will leverage unique human sample sets from biobanks and ongoing studies, as well as advanced technical approaches to study adaptive immunity, and a strong collaborative environment including local and international partners. The ideal candidate for this position is curious, self-motivated, well organized, and team-oriented. They will be expected to make intellectual contributions to projects, work at an appropriate level of supported independence, and exhibit leadership. The fellow will enjoy ample career development opportunities including competing for institutional training grants and fellowship awards, mentoring junior lab members, and leveraging resources within the School of Medicine’s Office for Postdoctoral Education for writing, CV polishing, and more. In this position, the fellow will further their skills in study design, writing and oral presentation, and navigating team science. The fellow will grow their own scientific network, starting among our collaborators with diverse expertise who include entomologists, immunologists, virologists, molecular and evolutionary biologists, epidemiologists, statisticians, vaccinologists, and clinicians. JOB DESCRIPTION: Helps design and conduct research within a specified field while receiving advanced training from a designated Principal Investigator to enhance professional skills and research independence needed for pursuit of a career. The specific area of research in which the trainee is mentored is determined by the department and laboratory of the Postdoc. Designs and evaluates experiments. Develops new ideas that promote current research. Prepares and publishes scientific manuscripts under the direction of the Principal Investigator. May be responsible for operation of specific equipment. May teach techniques to others, train, and supervise research staff. Positions are temporary appointments as a research trainee. The initial appointment is for one year, renewal expected if progress is satisfactory and funds are available. Appointments cannot exceed five years. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: A doctoral degree or equivalent (Ph.D., M.D., ScD., D.V.M., DDS etc) in an appropriate field. Excellent scientific writing ability and strong oral communication skills. The ability to work effectively and collegially with colleagues. Additional qualifications as specified by the Principal Investigator. NOTE: This role will be granted the opportunity to work from home regularly but must be able to commute to Emory University on a flexible weekly schedule based upon business needs. Schedule is based on agreed upon guidelines of department. This role requires residency in the state of GA. Emory reserves the right to change remote work status with notice to employee. Additional Details Emory is an equal opportunity employer, and qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, protected veteran status or other characteristics protected by state or federal law. Emory University does not discriminate in admissions, educational programs, or employment, including recruitment, hiring, promotions, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits, and training. Students, faculty, and staff are assured of participation in university programs and in the use of facilities without such discrimination. Emory University complies with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, the Vietnam Era Veteran's Readjustment Assistance Act, and applicable executive orders, federal and state regulations regarding nondiscrimination, equal opportunity, and affirmative action (for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities). Inquiries regarding this policy should be directed to the Emory University Department of Equity and Civil Rights Compliance, 201 Dowman Drive, Administration Building, Atlanta, GA 30322. Telephone: 404-727-9867 (V) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). Emory University is committed to ensuring equal access and providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to seek a reasonable accommodation, please contact the Department of Accessibility Services at accessibility@emory.edu or call 404-727-9877 (Voice) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). We kindly ask that requests be made at least seven business days in advance to allow adequate time for coordination. Options Apply Now Apply Share Email this job to a friend Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Connect With Us! Not ready to apply? Connect with us for general consideration! Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://faculty-emory.icims.com/jobs/172199/post-doctoral-researcher/job": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Researcher",
      "description": "Post Doctoral Researcher Job Number 172199 Job Type Regular Full-Time Division School Of Medicine Department SOM: Neurology: Movement Disor Campus Location (For Posting) : Location US-GA-Atlanta Location : Name Woodruff Remote Work Classification Primarily On Campus Health and Safety Information Not Applicable Discover Your Career at Emory University Emory University is a leading research university that fosters excellence and attracts world-class talent to innovate today and prepare leaders for the future. We welcome candidates who can contribute to the excellence of our academic community. Description Postdoctoral research fellow - Human movement disorders electrophysiology and neural engineering We are seeking a postdoctoral researcher at the intersection of neuroscience, machine learning, and clinical neural engineering to join a collaborative team developing the next generation of intelligent deep brain stimulation (DBS) systems for movement disorders. Dr. Miocinovic is a movement disorders neurologist and a biomedical engineer with expertise in translational and clinical electrophysiology. Her laboratory at Emory University focuses on brain circuits involved in motor and cognitive behaviors in Parkinson’s disease and other movement disorders. We investigate how to treat these disorders by modulating neural activity, primarily with DBS, and work on developing next-generation neuromodulation approaches. The methods used in the lab include intracranial recordings during DBS surgeries and non-invasive (EEG, EMG, kinematic) recordings in the clinic. These approaches are enhanced by advanced imaging techniques, biophysical computational modeling, quantitative symptom monitoring, behavioral tasks, and machine learning. A postdoctoral fellow would spearhead a project to develop automated DBS programming platform using electrophysiologic signals recorded in the operating room and tested in the clinic and at home. The project is a close collaboration with Dr. Matthew Gombolay at Georgia Tech’s School of Computing, and involves real-time deployment of machine learning algorithms. This is an excellent opportunity for those interested in translational neural engineering. We offer cutting-edge scientific projects, attentive mentoring and multi-disciplinary collaborations in a world-class research environment at Emory University and Georgia Institute of Technology. Additional information at Miocinovic Lab website: movement.bme.gatech.edu The position is available immediately QUALIFICATIONS: Required: ● PhD in Neuroscience, Biomedical Engineering, Neural Engineering or related disciplines ● Experience with neurophysiological studies ● Advanced quantitative signal processing skills (including expertise in Matlab or Python) ● Strong scientific writing ability and oral communication skills ● Ability to spearhead complex experiments in clinical environments in close collaboration with neurologists and neurosurgeons ● Record of first-author publications in peer-reviewed journals Preferred: ● Experience with human electrophysiology ● Experience with machine learning algorithms Apply by sending the following to Dr. Miocinovic (svjetlana.miocinovic@emory.edu) ● Current CV including all publications ● Cover letter describing interest and relevant experience for the project ● Three reference contacts NOTE: Position tasks are generally required to be performed in-person at an Emory University location. Remote work from home day options may be granted at department discretion. Emory reserves the right to change remote work status with notice to employee. Additional Details Emory is an equal opportunity employer, and qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, protected veteran status or other characteristics protected by state or federal law. Emory University does not discriminate in admissions, educational programs, or employment, including recruitment, hiring, promotions, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits, and training. Students, faculty, and staff are assured of participation in university programs and in the use of facilities without such discrimination. Emory University complies with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, the Vietnam Era Veteran's Readjustment Assistance Act, and applicable executive orders, federal and state regulations regarding nondiscrimination, equal opportunity, and affirmative action (for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities). Inquiries regarding this policy should be directed to the Emory University Department of Equity and Civil Rights Compliance, 201 Dowman Drive, Administration Building, Atlanta, GA 30322. Telephone: 404-727-9867 (V) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). Emory University is committed to ensuring equal access and providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to seek a reasonable accommodation, please contact the Department of Accessibility Services at accessibility@emory.edu or call 404-727-9877 (Voice) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). We kindly ask that requests be made at least seven business days in advance to allow adequate time for coordination. Options Apply Now Apply Share Email this job to a friend Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Connect With Us! Not ready to apply? Connect with us for general consideration! Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://faculty-emory.icims.com/jobs/172183/pediatric-neuropsychologist---outpatient-generalist-and-complex-epilepsy/job": {
      "title": "Pediatric Neuropsychologist - Outpatient Generalist and Complex Epilepsy",
      "description": "Pediatric Neuropsychologist - Outpatient Generalist and Complex Epilepsy Job Number 172183 Job Type Regular Full-Time Division Emory+Children's Ped Institute Department SOMPI: Neuropsychology Campus Location (For Posting) : Location US-GA-Atlanta Location : Name Emory Campus-Clifton Corridor Remote Work Classification No Remote Health and Safety Information Position involves clinical patient contact, Working with human blood, body fluids, tissues, or other potentially infectious materials Discover Your Career at Emory University Emory University is a leading research university that fosters excellence and attracts world-class talent to innovate today and prepare leaders for the future. We welcome candidates who can contribute to the excellence of our academic community. Description Oversees and develops neurofunctional assessments, including neuropsychological and psychological batteries. Assists with translating the findings from these batteries into therapeutic approaches which can be utilized by a transdisciplinary or interdisciplinary team. Participates in the supervision of students/residents. Counsels and educates families concerning neuropathology and management of neuropsychological deficits. Assists with the coordination of a clinical database and promotes research. Assists in the development of policies and procedures to provide the highest quality of care for patients within the scope of practice and established standards for psychologists. Maintains the highest standards of safety throughout service provision. Cooperates and collaborates with parents, caretakers, and other professionals to meet the total needs of the patient and family. Experience Special training and/or experience in pediatric neuropsychology 4 years of experience in direct assessment and care of patients identified as having neurological diagnoses in an inpatient and outpatient setting Experience in providing family therapy/counseling related to issues of impaired neurofunctioning Preferred Qualifications N/A Education Doctor of Philosophy or Doctor of Psychology Certification Summary Georgia licensed neuropsychologist or eligible with proof of application Basic Life Support (BLS) within 30 days of employment Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Excellent writing and research skills Ability to supervise students Job Responsibilities Provides effective delivery of services appropriate to the patient’s specific needs and severity level of disability or impairment. Implements and oversees inpatient cognitive remediation techniques. Provides appropriate assessment and appropriate treatment. Develops and successfully implements assessment standards and treatment plans that support established goals. Documents weekly/monthly treatment notes following established standards. Completes discharge and follows up according to departmental standards. Demonstrates commitment to continuous professional growth and improvement. Bills appropriately for services in accordance with regulations set forth by the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services. Provides supervision to the psychometrician and students. Supports and participates in continuous assessment and improvement of the quality of care and services provided. Delivers competent patient care, integrating infection control and body substance precautions, patient safety, body mechanics, and knowledge of growth and development. Delivers competent patient care to patients from newborn to 21 years of age. Demonstrates and practices family-centered care. Maintains a therapeutic relationship with patient and family. Communicates relevant information at the patient/family level of understanding. Identifies patient/family need for services from ancillary departments and provides follow-up and referral as necessary. Includes family/caregiver participation in all phases of the patient’s program. Available position Outpatient Generalist and Complex Epilepsy (1.0 clinical FTE): This neuropsychologist will conduct outpatient evaluations as well as support our epilepsy team, predominantly at the Scottish Rite campus. Experience conducting epilepsy-specific assessments and procedures (pre- and post-surgical evaluations, fMRI, Wada, cortical mapping, SEEG) is highly desired. Opportunities for providing consultative services in outpatient medical clinics is also possible. Select candidates will be eligible for an academic appointment at the Emory University School of Medicine, with rank commensurate with qualifications and experience. In addition, there are opportunities to engage in collaborative research. Consultative services in medical clinics are also possible. The pediatric neuropsychologist will have the opportunity to supervise trainees, including doctoral level graduate students (externs), interns, and fellows as part of our APPCN-affiliated postdoctoral fellowship and APA-accredited internship. Our site is committed to providing resources for individuals from diverse backgrounds with bilingual (Spanish/English) providers and staff. Bilingual applicants are encouraged to apply. Neuropsychology at Children's We have one of the leading neuropsychology practices in the Southeast including neuropsychologists and psychometrists. We see patients with complex medical and neurological histories throughout development (birth to age 21) in different settings (inpatient, outpatient, clinics) at different time points (acute, chronic) covering various subspecialties. Primary referral sources include Neurology, Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation, Neurosurgery and Cardiology. Common diagnoses include acquired injuries (TBI, anoxic brain injury, stroke, abusive head trauma [AHT]), new onset illness (meningitis/encephalitis, brain tumors), as well as various neurodevelopmental and chronic neurological conditions (cerebral palsy, spina bifida, epilepsy). NOTE: Position tasks are required to be performed in-person at an Emory University location; working remote is not an option. Emory reserves the right to change this status with notice to employee. Additional Details Emory is an equal opportunity employer, and qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, protected veteran status or other characteristics protected by state or federal law. Emory University does not discriminate in admissions, educational programs, or employment, including recruitment, hiring, promotions, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits, and training. Students, faculty, and staff are assured of participation in university programs and in the use of facilities without such discrimination. Emory University complies with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, the Vietnam Era Veteran's Readjustment Assistance Act, and applicable executive orders, federal and state regulations regarding nondiscrimination, equal opportunity, and affirmative action (for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities). Inquiries regarding this policy should be directed to the Emory University Department of Equity and Civil Rights Compliance, 201 Dowman Drive, Administration Building, Atlanta, GA 30322. Telephone: 404-727-9867 (V) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). Emory University is committed to ensuring equal access and providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to seek a reasonable accommodation, please contact the Department of Accessibility Services at accessibility@emory.edu or call 404-727-9877 (Voice) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). We kindly ask that requests be made at least seven business days in advance to allow adequate time for coordination. Options Apply Now Apply Share Email this job to a friend Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Connect With Us! Not ready to apply? Connect with us for general consideration! Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://faculty-emory.icims.com/jobs/172097/post-doctoral-fellow/job": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Post Doctoral Fellow Job Number 172097 Job Type Regular Full-Time Division School Of Public Health Department SPH: Global Health Campus Location (For Posting) : Location US-GA-Atlanta Location : Name Emory Campus-Clifton Corridor Required Documents Biosketch, Cover letter Remote Work Classification Hybrid Remote Health and Safety Information Not Applicable Discover Your Career at Emory University Emory University is a leading research university that fosters excellence and attracts world-class talent to innovate today and prepare leaders for the future. We welcome candidates who can contribute to the excellence of our academic community. Description Multiple postdoctoral positions are available with the Emory Global Diabetes Research Center (EGDRC; www.diabetes.emory.edu ) and the Georgia Center for Diabetes Translation Research (GCDTR; www.gcdtr.org ) at Emory University in Atlanta, USA. EGDRC and GCDTR have extensive U.S. and global collaborations spanning diabetes, cardiovascular disease (CVD), brain health and cognitive aging, and related chronic diseases. JOB DETAILS: Fellows will engage in innovative observational, experimental, and translational research focused on the biology, technology, prevention, and precision medicine of diabetes and related cardiometabolic conditions in the United States and globally. Opportunities also include investigating the relationships among diabetes, cardiometabolic health, cognitive aging, and other brain-related outcomes. Fellows will work with national and international teams to develop research questions, write grants and manuscripts, design studies, and conduct rigorous analyses of complex data from population-based cohorts and surveillance studies, electronic health records, large surveys, and clinical and community-based intervention trials. Available data span multiple dimensions, including imaging, cognitive assessments, biomarkers, genomics, wearable and sensor data, and other digital health data streams. Research responsibilities may include developing statistical analysis plans, harmonizing data across studies and cohorts, and applying state-of-the-art epidemiologic, statistical, and computational methods to longitudinal and high-dimensional data. Depending on their interests and expertise, Fellows may apply AI/ML, deep learning, and other data science approaches and participate in clinical and pragmatic trials and implementation science projects. Additional opportunities include developing study protocols and research proposals, designing data collection tools and digital health studies, mentoring junior researchers, and leading peer-reviewed publications and scientific presentations. Fellows will work with faculty including Drs. Venkat Narayan, Allan Levey, Mohammed Ali, Felicia Goldstein, Ram Jagannathan, and Shivani Patel, as well as other investigators across Emory and collaborating institutions. They will also have opportunities to collaborate with experts in AI/ML, population genomics, and computational methods, including Drs. Anant Madabhushi, Yan Sun, and Joyce Ho. The positions offer strong opportunities for mentorship, interdisciplinary collaboration, career development, and independent research. Emory's unique partnerships with the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, Georgia Institute of Technology, CARE, the Carter Center, the Task Force for Global Health, and research and global health organizations worldwide provide an exceptional environment for interdisciplinary research and training. The Rollins School of Public Health includes six academic departments and numerous interdisciplinary research centers and programs. JOB DESCRIPTION: Helps design and conduct research within a specified field while receiving advanced training from a designated Principal Investigator to enhance professional skills and research independence needed for pursuit of a career. The specific area of research in which the trainee is mentored is determined by the department and laboratory of the Postdoc. Designs and evaluates experiments. Develops new ideas that promote current research. Prepares and publishes scientific manuscripts under the direction of the Principal Investigator. May be responsible for operation of specific equipment. May teach techniques to others, train, and supervise research staff. Positions are temporary appointments as a research trainee. The initial appointment is for one year, renewal expected if progress is satisfactory and funds are available. Appointments cannot exceed five years. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: A doctoral degree or equivalent (Ph.D., M.D., ScD., D.V.M., DDS etc) in an appropriate field. Excellent scientific writing ability and strong oral communication skills. The ability to work effectively and collegially with colleagues. Additional qualifications as specified by the Principal Investigator. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: We seek candidates with an MD and/or PhD in epidemiology, biostatistics, nutrition, behavioral sciences, demography, computer science, data science, biomedical informatics, medicine, or a related field. Candidates should have strong scientific writing, quantitative, statistical, and programming skills, with experience analyzing complex longitudinal data, particularly electronic health records, cohort studies, and large survey datasets. Experience developing peer-reviewed manuscripts is highly desirable. Substantive knowledge of diabetes, obesity, CVD, cognitive aging, brain health, or social determinants of health is a plus. Experience or strong interest in AI/ML, digital health technologies, wearable devices, continuous glucose monitoring (CGM), or other computational methods is highly desirable. Expertise in—or enthusiasm for learning and engaging in—clinical trials, pragmatic trials, and implementation science will also be viewed favorably. Candidates should demonstrate the ability to work independently and collaboratively; strong organizational skills; excellent written and verbal communication skills in English; and enthusiasm for interdisciplinary research spanning epidemiology, medicine, neuroscience, computer science, engineering, and data science. NOTE: This role will be granted the opportunity to work from home regularly but must be able to commute to Emory University on a flexible weekly schedule based upon business needs. Schedule is based on agreed upon guidelines of department. This role requires residency in the state of GA. Emory reserves the right to change remote work status with notice to employee. Additional Details Emory is an equal opportunity employer, and qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, protected veteran status or other characteristics protected by state or federal law. Emory University does not discriminate in admissions, educational programs, or employment, including recruitment, hiring, promotions, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits, and training. Students, faculty, and staff are assured of participation in university programs and in the use of facilities without such discrimination. Emory University complies with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, the Vietnam Era Veteran's Readjustment Assistance Act, and applicable executive orders, federal and state regulations regarding nondiscrimination, equal opportunity, and affirmative action (for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities). Inquiries regarding this policy should be directed to the Emory University Department of Equity and Civil Rights Compliance, 201 Dowman Drive, Administration Building, Atlanta, GA 30322. Telephone: 404-727-9867 (V) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). Emory University is committed to ensuring equal access and providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to seek a reasonable accommodation, please contact the Department of Accessibility Services at accessibility@emory.edu or call 404-727-9877 (Voice) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). We kindly ask that requests be made at least seven business days in advance to allow adequate time for coordination. Options Apply Now Apply Share Email this job to a friend Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Connect With Us! Not ready to apply? Connect with us for general consideration! Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://faculty-emory.icims.com/jobs/172095/post-doctoral-fellow/job": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Post Doctoral Fellow Job Number 172095 Job Type Regular Full-Time Division School Of Medicine Department SOM: HMO: Hematology Campus Location (For Posting) : Location US-GA-Atlanta Location : Name HSRB II (Health Sciences Research Building II) Remote Work Classification No Remote Health and Safety Information Working in a laboratory, Working with animals, Working with human blood, body fluids, tissues, or other potentially infectious materials Discover Your Career at Emory University Emory University is a leading research university that fosters excellence and attracts world-class talent to innovate today and prepare leaders for the future. We welcome candidates who can contribute to the excellence of our academic community. Description Bhatt Lab, Winship Cancer Institute, Emory Non-genetic mechanisms of therapy resistance in leukemia | NCI R37-funded We're looking for a postdoc to join our team studying how leukemia cells dodge therapy through non-genetic and epigenetic adaptation (drug-tolerant persister states). The position will be funded through the new award - NCI R37 MERIT Award. Bhatt lab ( https://www.bhatt-lab.com/) is based at Winship Cancer Institute with a joint home in the GT–Emory Coulter Department of Biomedical Engineering, so there's a good mix of hematology, engineering, and computational folks around. What we're looking for: Fresh PhD grads with no more than 1-2 years of postdoc experience Solid wet-lab chops, and genuinely comfortable with epigenetics (ChIP-seq, ATAC-seq, chromatin stuff) Not afraid of computational/genomic data — you don't need to be a bioinformatician, but you should be able to dig into sequencing datasets and work with people who are Some evidence you can write and finish projects (papers, preprints, a solid thesis) Someone who's independent but also likes being part of a team JOB DESCRIPTION: Helps design and conduct research within a specified field while receiving advanced training from a designated Principal Investigator to enhance professional skills and research independence needed for pursuit of a career. The specific area of research in which the trainee is mentored is determined by the department and laboratory of the Postdoc. Designs and evaluates experiments. Develops new ideas that promote current research. Prepares and publishes scientific manuscripts under the direction of the Principal Investigator. May be responsible for operation of specific equipment. May teach techniques to others, train, and supervise research staff. Positions are temporary appointments as a research trainee. The initial appointment is for one year, renewal expected if progress is satisfactory and funds are available. Appointments cannot exceed five years. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: A doctoral degree or equivalent (Ph.D., M.D., ScD., D.V.M., DDS etc) in an appropriate field. Excellent scientific writing ability and strong oral communication skills. The ability to work effectively and collegially with colleagues. Additional qualifications as specified by the Principal Investigator. NOTE: Position tasks are required to be performed in-person at an Emory University location; working remote is not an option. Emory reserves the right to change this status with notice to employee. Additional Details Emory is an equal opportunity employer, and qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, protected veteran status or other characteristics protected by state or federal law. Emory University does not discriminate in admissions, educational programs, or employment, including recruitment, hiring, promotions, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits, and training. Students, faculty, and staff are assured of participation in university programs and in the use of facilities without such discrimination. Emory University complies with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, the Vietnam Era Veteran's Readjustment Assistance Act, and applicable executive orders, federal and state regulations regarding nondiscrimination, equal opportunity, and affirmative action (for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities). Inquiries regarding this policy should be directed to the Emory University Department of Equity and Civil Rights Compliance, 201 Dowman Drive, Administration Building, Atlanta, GA 30322. Telephone: 404-727-9867 (V) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). Emory University is committed to ensuring equal access and providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to seek a reasonable accommodation, please contact the Department of Accessibility Services at accessibility@emory.edu or call 404-727-9877 (Voice) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). We kindly ask that requests be made at least seven business days in advance to allow adequate time for coordination. Options Apply Now Apply Share Email this job to a friend Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Connect With Us! Not ready to apply? Connect with us for general consideration! Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://faculty-emory.icims.com/jobs/171964/post-doctoral-fellow-%7c-neh-postdoctoral-fellowship-in-poetics-2027-2028/job": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Fellow | NEH Postdoctoral Fellowship in Poetics 2027-2028",
      "description": "Post Doctoral Fellow | NEH Postdoctoral Fellowship in Poetics 2027-2028 Job Number 171964 Job Type Regular Full-Time Division Emory College Department ECAS: Ctr Humanistic Inquiry Campus Location (For Posting) : Location US-GA-Atlanta Location : Name Emory Campus-Clifton Corridor Required Documents Cover letter, List of 3 Recommenders Remote Work Classification Primarily On Campus Health and Safety Information Not Applicable Discover Your Career at Emory University Emory University is a leading research university that fosters excellence and attracts world-class talent to innovate today and prepare leaders for the future. We welcome candidates who can contribute to the excellence of our academic community. Description +++ To learn more about this position and to apply, please visit https://apply.interfolio.com/190917 +++ JOB DETAILS: The Fox Center for Humanistic Inquiry is pleased to open applications for our NEH Postdoctoral Fellowship in Poetics. This longstanding fellowship reflects the importance of Emory’s Raymond Danowski Poetry Library, part of the Stuart A. Rose Manuscripts, Archives and Rare Book Library, as a center for research in poetry. We are especially eager to support research projects which make use of Emory University's archival holdings in poetry. We invite applications from scholars who are eager to be part of a community engaged in interdisciplinary conversations around our theme of Humanities Plural. (See below.) Research projects do not need to address the theme directly, but applicants should briefly address how the project relates to significant methodological, theoretical, or conceptual issues in the humanities. (For example, a project in an established scholarly tradition could make a case for the importance of carrying that tradition forward within the current humanities landscape; a project in an emerging area of scholarship could share some thoughts about interdisciplinarity or new methods.) The Fox Center will appoint one NEH Postdoctoral Fellow in Poetics for the academic year 2027-28. The appointment will be for one year (August 1, 2027-July 31, 2028), with the possibility of renewal for one additional year. The fellowship includes a stipend of $62,000, a research budget of $2,000, and a one-time additional payment of $2,000 to defray the costs associated with moving to Atlanta. Fellows are eligible for a wide range of competitive benefits, including dental and vision care, and are required to be in residence for the term of the fellowship. All postdoctoral fellows participate in a weekly interdisciplinary seminar, presenting their research at one of these meetings, and teach one undergraduate class in their area of expertise in the College of Arts and Sciences. If invited to continue for a second year, fellows will have additional teaching responsibilities and will receive teaching mentorship from Emory faculty in a relevant discipline. The application deadline for applicants to submit their materials is December 7, 2026, at 11:59 PM ET. The deadline for receipt of letters of recommendation is December 11, 2026, at 11.59 PM ET. JOB DESCRIPTION: Helps design and conduct research within a specified field while receiving advanced training from a designated Principal Investigator to enhance professional skills and research independence needed for pursuit of a career. The specific area of research in which the trainee is mentored is determined by the department and laboratory of the Postdoc. Designs and evaluates experiments. Develops new ideas that promote current research. Prepares and publishes scientific manuscripts under the direction of the Principal Investigator. May be responsible for operation of specific equipment. May teach techniques to others, train, and supervise research staff. Positions are temporary appointments as a research trainee. The initial appointment is for one year, renewal expected if progress is satisfactory and funds are available. Appointments cannot exceed five years. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: A doctoral degree or equivalent (Ph.D., M.D., ScD., D.V.M., DDS etc) in an appropriate field. Excellent scientific writing ability and strong oral communication skills. The ability to work effectively and collegially with colleagues. Additional qualifications as specified by the Principal Investigator. QUALIFICATION DETAILS: All postdoctoral fellows must hold a PhD (or its international equivalent, such as the DPhil). Applicants may not have received their doctorate before January 1, 2024. Preference will be given to applicants who have not held prior postdoctoral fellowships. Applicants applying without a degree in hand must have successfully completed all degree requirements by June 30, 2027. Scholars from outside the United States are appointed under either the J-1 visa (Research Scholar status) or F-1 OPT (Optional Practical Training), depending on their circumstances. The Fox Center reserves the right to cancel awards if the recipient is unable to meet these conditions of completion and visa status. +++ To learn more about this position and to apply, please visit https://apply.interfolio.com/190917 +++ NOTE: Position tasks are generally required to be performed in-person at an Emory University location. Remote work from home day options may be granted at department discretion. Emory reserves the right to change remote work status with notice to employee. Additional Details Emory is an equal opportunity employer, and qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, protected veteran status or other characteristics protected by state or federal law. Emory University does not discriminate in admissions, educational programs, or employment, including recruitment, hiring, promotions, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits, and training. Students, faculty, and staff are assured of participation in university programs and in the use of facilities without such discrimination. Emory University complies with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, the Vietnam Era Veteran's Readjustment Assistance Act, and applicable executive orders, federal and state regulations regarding nondiscrimination, equal opportunity, and affirmative action (for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities). Inquiries regarding this policy should be directed to the Emory University Department of Equity and Civil Rights Compliance, 201 Dowman Drive, Administration Building, Atlanta, GA 30322. Telephone: 404-727-9867 (V) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). Emory University is committed to ensuring equal access and providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to seek a reasonable accommodation, please contact the Department of Accessibility Services at accessibility@emory.edu or call 404-727-9877 (Voice) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). We kindly ask that requests be made at least seven business days in advance to allow adequate time for coordination. Options Apply Now Apply Share Email this job to a friend Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Connect With Us! Not ready to apply? Connect with us for general consideration! Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://faculty-emory.icims.com/jobs/171924/post-doctoral-fellow-%7c-mellon-postdoctoral-fellowship-2027-2028%3a-humanities-plural/job": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Fellow | Mellon Postdoctoral Fellowship 2027-2028: Humanities Plural",
      "description": "Post Doctoral Fellow | Mellon Postdoctoral Fellowship 2027-2028: Humanities Plural Job Number 171924 Job Type Regular Full-Time Division Emory College Department ECAS: Ctr Humanistic Inquiry Campus Location (For Posting) : Location US-GA-Atlanta Location : Name Emory Campus-Clifton Corridor Required Documents Cover letter, List of 3 Recommenders Remote Work Classification Primarily On Campus Health and Safety Information Not Applicable Discover Your Career at Emory University Emory University is a leading research university that fosters excellence and attracts world-class talent to innovate today and prepare leaders for the future. We welcome candidates who can contribute to the excellence of our academic community. Description ++ To learn more about this position and to apply, please visit https://apply.interfolio.com/190909 +++ JOB DETAILS: The Fox Center for Humanistic Inquiry is pleased to open applications for our Mellon Postdoctoral Fellowships. At a moment when fields such as \"history of the humanities\" and \"humanities theory\" are coming into view, and as hybrid formations such as digital humanities, health humanities, and public humanities are taking humanities scholarship into new academic and extra-academic spaces, we seek scholars whose research expands our horizons for thinking about the humanities as they have existed or are unfolding in our current moment. Applications are open to scholars in any field, area, or historical period. Projects need not explicitly foreground \"the humanities\" (or related formations such as \"liberal arts\") as an object of inquiry, but they should clearly address any core issue of humanities scholarship or learning, including its methods, its objects, its histories, its values and social valuation, its contradictions, its publics, or its institutional or social locations. Potential lines of inquiry include: -What are the contexts, across time and space, where humanistic learning occurs and matters (e.g., formal or informal academic settings, places of worship, prisons, online spaces, museums)? - Where is humanistic scholarship enlisted into serving other systems of knowledge and value (e.g., medical schools, think tanks, revolutionary movements, corporate boardrooms), and what sorts of work is it asked to do there? - Which communities have forged programs or practices of humanistic learning without having access to formal or elite institutional structures? What are the distinct methods, epistemologies, aesthetics, and/or values that take shape in these sites? - What gave rise to \"theory,\" where and why has it taken root, and what is its future? - How do or should humanistic methods function in hybrid formations, for example in digital humanities, environmental humanities, or health humanities? The Fox Center will appoint two Mellon Postdoctoral Fellows for one year (August 1, 2027-July 31, 2028), with the possibility of renewal for one additional year. Fellowships include a stipend of $62,000, a research budget of $2,000, and a one-time additional payment of $2,000 to defray the costs associated with moving to Atlanta, and competitive benefits including medical, dental, and vision care. Fellows are required to be in residence during the academic year for the term of the fellowship. All postdoctoral fellows participate in a weekly interdisciplinary seminar, presenting their research at one of these meetings, and teach one undergraduate class in their area of expertise in the College of Arts and Sciences. If invited to continue for a second year, fellows will have additional teaching responsibilities and will receive teaching mentorship from Emory faculty in a relevant discipline. The application deadline for applicants to submit their materials is December 7, 2026, at 11:59 PM ET. The deadline for receipt of letters of recommendation is December 11, 2026, at 11.59 PM ET. JOB DESCRIPTION: Helps design and conduct research within a specified field while receiving advanced training from a designated Principal Investigator to enhance professional skills and research independence needed for pursuit of a career. The specific area of research in which the trainee is mentored is determined by the department and laboratory of the Postdoc. Designs and evaluates experiments. Develops new ideas that promote current research. Prepares and publishes scientific manuscripts under the direction of the Principal Investigator. May be responsible for operation of specific equipment. May teach techniques to others, train, and supervise research staff. Positions are temporary appointments as a research trainee. The initial appointment is for one year, renewal expected if progress is satisfactory and funds are available. Appointments cannot exceed five years. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: A doctoral degree or equivalent (Ph.D., M.D., ScD., D.V.M., DDS etc) in an appropriate field. Excellent scientific writing ability and strong oral communication skills. The ability to work effectively and collegially with colleagues. Additional qualifications as specified by the Principal Investigator. QUALIFICATIONS DETAILS: All postdoctoral fellows must hold a PhD (or its international equivalent, such as the DPhil). Applicants may not have received their doctorate before January 1, 2024. Preference will be given to applicants who have not held prior postdoctoral fellowships. Applicants applying without a degree in hand must have successfully completed all degree requirements by June 30, 2027. Scholars from outside the United States are appointed under either the J-1 visa (Research Scholar status) or F-1 OPT (Optional Practical Training), depending on their circumstances. The Fox Center reserves the right to cancel awards if the recipient is unable to meet these conditions of completion and visa status. +++ To learn more about this position and to apply, please visit https://apply.interfolio.com/190909 +++ NOTE: Position tasks are generally required to be performed in-person at an Emory University location. Remote work from home day options may be granted at department discretion. Emory reserves the right to change remote work status with notice to employee. Additional Details Emory is an equal opportunity employer, and qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, protected veteran status or other characteristics protected by state or federal law. Emory University does not discriminate in admissions, educational programs, or employment, including recruitment, hiring, promotions, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits, and training. Students, faculty, and staff are assured of participation in university programs and in the use of facilities without such discrimination. Emory University complies with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, the Vietnam Era Veteran's Readjustment Assistance Act, and applicable executive orders, federal and state regulations regarding nondiscrimination, equal opportunity, and affirmative action (for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities). Inquiries regarding this policy should be directed to the Emory University Department of Equity and Civil Rights Compliance, 201 Dowman Drive, Administration Building, Atlanta, GA 30322. Telephone: 404-727-9867 (V) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). Emory University is committed to ensuring equal access and providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to seek a reasonable accommodation, please contact the Department of Accessibility Services at accessibility@emory.edu or call 404-727-9877 (Voice) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). We kindly ask that requests be made at least seven business days in advance to allow adequate time for coordination. Options Apply Now Apply Share Email this job to a friend Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Connect With Us! Not ready to apply? Connect with us for general consideration! Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://faculty-emory.icims.com/jobs/171904/simulation-nurse-educator/job": {
      "title": "Simulation Nurse Educator",
      "description": "Simulation Nurse Educator Job Number 171904 Job Type Regular Full-Time Division School Of Nursing Department SON:Clinical & Community Pract Campus Location (For Posting) : Location US-GA-Atlanta Location : Name Emory Campus-Clifton Corridor Required Documents Cover letter, Letter of Interest, List of 3 Recommenders Remote Work Classification No Remote Health and Safety Information Not Applicable Discover Your Career at Emory University Emory University is a leading research university that fosters excellence and attracts world-class talent to innovate today and prepare leaders for the future. We welcome candidates who can contribute to the excellence of our academic community. Description The Nell Hodgson Woodruff School of Nursing is seeking a Simulation Nurse Educator to support high-quality simulation-based education, faculty development, learner support, and continuous program improvement. The Simulation Nurse Educator serves as an educational consultant and primary support resource for an assigned portfolio of courses. Working collaboratively with course faculty, simulation coordinators, clinical instructors, simulation operations personnel, and School of Nursing leadership, this individual helps ensure simulation activities align with course and program outcomes, evidence-based standards, accreditation expectations, and institutional priorities. The position also supports open laboratory learning, clinical remediation, faculty and facilitator development, simulated participant training, educational evaluation, accreditation readiness, and dissemination of educational innovations through scholarship and professional contributions. Responsibilities Serve as the primary simulation education contact for assigned courses. Consult with faculty on simulation design, learner preparation, facilitation, debriefing, evaluation, and continuous improvement. Review simulation activities for educational readiness and alignment with course objectives, learner needs, and established simulation standards. Provide faculty and clinical instructors with clinical skills refreshers, coaching, and instructional guidance. Educate and support faculty, clinical instructors, and simulated participants in planning, facilitating, debriefing, and evaluating simulation-based learning activities. Develop, facilitate, and evaluate open laboratory and supplemental learning experiences. Provide individualized clinical remediation and learner support in collaboration with faculty and academic leadership. Mentor and coach faculty and clinical instructors who are new to simulation-based education. Support required simulation documentation, outcomes review, quality improvement, and accreditation activities. Collaborate with simulation operations personnel to ensure educational requirements are complete before implementation. Use learner, facilitator, and program data to guide educational improvement. Promote the impact of simulation education and related initiatives through presentations, publications, scholarship, quality improvement, and other professional contributions. Minimum Qualifications Master’s degree in Nursing. Current unencumbered Registered Nurse license. Minimum of three years of experience in nursing education, clinical education, staff development, simulation-based education, or academic leadership. Knowledge of adult learning principles and nursing education best practices. Experience supporting learners, coaching faculty, and contributing to educational evaluation or quality improvement. Excellent communication, organizational, collaboration, and project-management skills. Preferred Qualifications Doctoral degree in Nursing, Education, or a related field. Certified Healthcare Simulation Educator (CHSE) certification or eligibility to obtain certification; certification must be earned within two years of hire. Experience with healthcare simulation standards, accreditation, curriculum development, competency assessment, remediation, or faculty development. Knowledge of the INACSL Healthcare Simulation Standards of Best Practice. Experience supporting interprofessional or multi-program simulation activities. Experience with educational data, learning management, survey, or project-management systems. NOTE: Position tasks are required to be performed in person at the Emory Nursing Learning Center (ENLC); working remotely is not an option. Emory reserves the right to change this status with notice to the employee. Additional Details Emory is an equal opportunity employer, and qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, protected veteran status or other characteristics protected by state or federal law. Emory University does not discriminate in admissions, educational programs, or employment, including recruitment, hiring, promotions, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits, and training. Students, faculty, and staff are assured of participation in university programs and in the use of facilities without such discrimination. Emory University complies with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, the Vietnam Era Veteran's Readjustment Assistance Act, and applicable executive orders, federal and state regulations regarding nondiscrimination, equal opportunity, and affirmative action (for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities). Inquiries regarding this policy should be directed to the Emory University Department of Equity and Civil Rights Compliance, 201 Dowman Drive, Administration Building, Atlanta, GA 30322. Telephone: 404-727-9867 (V) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). Emory University is committed to ensuring equal access and providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to seek a reasonable accommodation, please contact the Department of Accessibility Services at accessibility@emory.edu or call 404-727-9877 (Voice) | 404-712-2049 (TDD). We kindly ask that requests be made at least seven business days in advance to allow adequate time for coordination. Options Apply Now Apply Share Email this job to a friend Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Connect With Us! Not ready to apply? Connect with us for general consideration! Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
