    "https://faculty-emory.icims.com/jobs/170502/headache-medicine-physician/job": {
      "title": "Headache Medicine Physician",
      "description": "Headache Medicine Physician Job Number 170502 Job Type Regular Full-Time Division The Emory Clinic Department TEC Neurology Headache HBC Campus Location (For Posting) : Location US-GA-Atlanta Location : Name Executive Park 12 Remote Work Classification Primarily On Campus Health and Safety Information Position involves clinical patient contact, Working with human blood, body fluids, tissues, or other potentially infectious materials Discover Your Career at Emory University Emory University is a leading research university that fosters excellence and attracts world-class talent to innovate today and prepare leaders for the future. We welcome candidates who can contribute to the excellence of our academic community. Description Join our growing team at the Emory Comprehensive Headache Center. Both academic and private practice tracks are available, so you can choose the path that fits your career goals. You'll practice in a dedicated outpatient headache clinic with full support for advanced procedures, including: OnabotulinumtoxinA (Botox) therapy Trigger-point injections Nerve blocks Outpatient infusions You'll also work closely with Emory's subspecialty teams in neuroradiology, neuro-ophthalmology, sleep medicine, cognitive neurology, neurosurgery and psychiatry. This position offers competitive compensation, comprehensive benefits, and outstanding opportunities for professional growth, academic advancement, and leadership. Join us in advancing the care of patients with headache disorders while helping shape the future of Headache Medicine at Emory. Qualifications: MD or DO degree and completed fellowship training in Headache Medicine. Additional Details Emory is an equal opportunity employer, and qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, protected veteran status or other characteristics protected by state or federal law. Emory University does not di